% 38d-emit-x86.weft — The second target: an x86-64 / Mach-O back end that
% emits textual assembly and delegates encoding to the system assembler.

\chapter{A Second Target: x86-64 by Assembly Emission}
\label{ch:emit-x86}

The flagship ARM64 back end (Chapters~\ref{ch:emit-arm64}--\ref{ch:emit-macho})
proves \kcc{} can go all the way to the metal: it encodes every instruction
into its own bytes and lays out the Mach-O container by hand.  The second
target takes the other road the porting playbook
(Chapter~\ref{ch:retargeting}) sanctions---\emph{assembly emission}.  Instead
of encoding instructions and writing an object file, this back end walks the
same target-neutral IR the ARM64 path consumes and \emph{prints x86-64
assembly text}, then hands that text to the platform assembler
(\texttt{as -arch x86\_64}).  The assembler does the encoding and produces the
Mach-O object; the linker (\texttt{ld -arch x86\_64}) produces an executable
that runs on Apple silicon under the Rosetta translator.  Nothing above the
emission step---the front end, the IR, the optimizer---knows or cares that a
different machine is now the destination; that is the whole point of the seam.

This chapter is built one rung of the playbook ladder at a time.  Rung~one
(\S\ref{sec:x86-rung1-history}) was the smallest program that exercises the
entire pipeline end to end, \verb|int main(void) { return N; }|, whose
optimized IR is a single \texttt{IR\_CONST} feeding an \texttt{IR\_RET}.  With
the scaffolding proven live, this chapter is \emph{rung~two}: the integer
arithmetic--logic unit plus the memory operations that give locals an address.
That is exactly the set of opcodes a function like

\begin{verbatim}
  int f(void) {
      volatile int x = 40;
      volatile int y = 2;
      return x + y;
  }
\end{verbatim}

\noindent lowers to---\texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} for each local, \texttt{IR\_STORE}
to initialise it, \texttt{IR\_LOAD} to read it back (\texttt{volatile} stops
\texttt{mem2reg} from promoting the locals into registers, so the memory
traffic survives to the back end), \texttt{IR\_ADD} to combine them, and an
\texttt{IR\_SEXT} feeding the return.  Rung~two selects for the whole integer
ALU (add, subtract, multiply, divide, remainder, the bitwise and shift
operators, negate and complement), the width-changing conversions
(\texttt{sext}/\texttt{zext}/\texttt{trunc}/\texttt{bitcast}), and the memory
opcodes (\texttt{alloca}/\texttt{load}/\texttt{store}/\texttt{gep}).

\emph{Rung~three} completes the scalar language: control flow and function
calls.  It adds the comparisons (\texttt{cmp\_eq} through \texttt{cmp\_ule}),
the branches (\texttt{br}/\texttt{br\_cond}), and the call machinery
(\texttt{call}/\texttt{call\_ind}/\texttt{global\_ref}) marshalled to the
System~V AMD64 ABI, along with the callee-side spill of incoming parameters.
With this rung a whole program of mutually recursive integer functions,
conditionals, and loops compiles and runs exit-identically to clang, provided
its data lives in \emph{functions}: parameters, locals, and calls between
defined functions all work, and an \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} to a defined
\emph{function} resolves (that is how an indirect call names its target).
What this rung deliberately leaves out is \emph{data emission}: it emits no
storage for file-scope variables, so a reference to a global object is an
unresolved symbol at link time rather than a miscompile.  That, together with
string-literal data, floating point, and by-value aggregates, is the work of a
later rung; an unrecognised opcode is still silently skipped.

\section{Strategy: an All-Spill Frame}
\label{sec:x86-strategy}

The x86-64 back end borrows the ARM64 code generator's ``all-spill'' strategy
(\S\ref{sec:codegen-strategy}) wholesale, because it is the simplest thing that
is correct and it sidesteps register allocation entirely.  \emph{Every virtual
register owns one eight-byte slot in the stack frame.}  An instruction loads
its operands from their slots into scratch registers, computes, and stores the
result back to the destination's slot.  No value is assumed to survive in a
register from one IR instruction to the next, so there is no allocation, no
liveness, and no spilling logic---just a fixed, mechanical lowering.

The frame is the conventional \texttt{\%rbp}-anchored one and has two regions
below the saved base pointer:

\begin{verbatim}
  0(%rbp)                 saved %rbp (pushq'd on entry)
  -8(%rbp) .. -vc*8(%rbp)  vreg value slots   (vreg_count * 8)
  below that              alloca storage      (alloca_slots * 8)
\end{verbatim}

Virtual register~$V$ lives at \verb|-(V+1)*8(%rbp)|.  Alloca storage begins
past the value slots so an \texttt{alloca}'s object can never overlap a vreg
slot (the same anti-aliasing discipline the ARM64 frame uses).  A pre-scan
assigns each \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} a running slot offset and sums the total; the
frame is \verb|align16((vreg_count + alloca_slots) * 8)| bytes, carved out with
one \texttt{subq} in the prologue and released by \texttt{leave} at each return.

Arithmetic runs at full 64-bit width, exactly as the ARM64 path does: operands
arrive in their slots already sign- or zero-extended (the loads and the
explicit conversion opcodes see to that), so a 64-bit \texttt{addq} computes
the correct low bits and the IR's own \texttt{sext}/\texttt{trunc} nodes
re-normalise a narrower result where the language requires it.  The scratch
registers are \verb|%r10| (first source and result), \verb|%r11| (second
source), and---only for the shift and divide instructions, which pin specific
registers---\verb|%rcx|, \verb|%rax|, and \verb|%rdx|.

\section{Target State and Assembly Vocabulary}
\label{sec:x86-data}

A single global, \texttt{\_kcc\_target}, records which back end the driver
selected: \texttt{0} (the default) is ARM64/Mach-O and leaves the flagship
path completely untouched; \texttt{1} is x86-64.  A second file-local global,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_vreg\_n}, holds the current function's virtual-register
count so instruction selection can size the frame and compute alloca
addresses.  The remaining data are the fixed fragments of AT\&T assembly this
back end pours into the text buffer---section and symbol directives, the frame
prologue/epilogue, every instruction mnemonic, and the register operands.
AT\&T syntax names registers with a \texttt{\%} sigil, immediates with
\texttt{\$}, orders operands source-then-destination, and writes a memory
operand as \verb|disp(base)|.

@d X86: data @{
.data
.globl _kcc_target
.globl _kcc_x86_tu
.globl _kcc_x86_os
_kcc_target:     .quad 0        // 0 = arm64/Mach-O (default), 1 = x86-64
_kcc_x86_vreg_n: .quad 0        // current function's vreg count (frame sizing)
_kcc_x86_tu:     .quad 0        // the AST translation unit (for data emission)
_kcc_x86_strs:   .quad 0        // vec of {data_ptr, len} string-literal entries
_kcc_x86_os:     .quad 0        // 0 = Darwin/Mach-O (default), 1 = Linux/ELF

.section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals
// Directives and symbol scaffolding.
_kcc_x86_s_text:     .asciz ".text\n"
_kcc_x86_s_globl:    .asciz ".globl "
_kcc_x86_s_us:       .asciz "_"

// Linux/ELF-flavour directives (selected when _kcc_x86_os = 1).
_kcc_x86_s_rodata:   .asciz ".section .rodata\n"
_kcc_x86_s_comm:     .asciz "\t.comm "
_kcc_x86_s_comm_a8:  .asciz ",8\n"          // ",<size>,8\n" .comm alignment tail

// Module-init pointer: the section header, then ".quad <name>\n" registers the
// synthesised kcc.module_init so it runs before main.  Darwin: __mod_init_func;
// Linux/ELF: .init_array.
_kcc_x86_s_modinit_d:.asciz ".section __DATA,__mod_init_func,mod_init_funcs\n\t.p2align 3\n\t.quad "
_kcc_x86_s_modinit_l:.asciz ".section .init_array,\"aw\"\n\t.p2align 3\n\t.quad "
_kcc_x86_s_modinitnm:.asciz "kcc.module_init"

// Read-only initialised data (const globals): section, alignment, and the
// value directives keyed by element width.  Darwin uses __TEXT,__const.
_kcc_x86_s_constsect:.asciz ".section __TEXT,__const\n"
_kcc_x86_s_p2align3: .asciz "\t.p2align 3\n"
_kcc_x86_s_d_byte:   .asciz "\t.byte "
_kcc_x86_s_d_short:  .asciz "\t.short "
_kcc_x86_s_d_long:   .asciz "\t.long "
_kcc_x86_s_d_quad:   .asciz "\t.quad "
_kcc_x86_s_zerodir:  .asciz "\t.zero "
_kcc_x86_s_colon_nl: .asciz ":\n"
_kcc_x86_s_minus:    .asciz "-"
_kcc_x86_s_rbp:      .asciz "(%rbp)"
_kcc_x86_s_comma:    .asciz ", "

// Frame prologue/epilogue.
_kcc_x86_s_push_rbp: .asciz "\tpushq\t%rbp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_mov_sp:   .asciz "\tmovq\t%rsp, %rbp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sub_sp:   .asciz "\tsubq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_sp_nl:    .asciz ", %rsp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_leave:    .asciz "\tleave\n"
_kcc_x86_s_retq:     .asciz "\tretq\n"

// Move family (with a trailing tab where an operand follows).
_kcc_x86_s_movq:     .asciz "\tmovq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movabsq:  .asciz "\tmovabsq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_movl:     .asciz "\tmovl\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movb:     .asciz "\tmovb\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movw:     .asciz "\tmovw\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movslq:   .asciz "\tmovslq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movsbq:   .asciz "\tmovsbq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movswq:   .asciz "\tmovswq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movzbq:   .asciz "\tmovzbq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movzwq:   .asciz "\tmovzwq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_leaq:     .asciz "\tleaq\t"

// Integer ALU mnemonics.
_kcc_x86_s_addq:     .asciz "\taddq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_subq:     .asciz "\tsubq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_imulq:    .asciz "\timulq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_andq:     .asciz "\tandq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_orq:      .asciz "\torq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_xorq:     .asciz "\txorq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_negq:     .asciz "\tnegq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_notq:     .asciz "\tnotq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_shlq:     .asciz "\tshlq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_sarq:     .asciz "\tsarq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_shrq:     .asciz "\tshrq\t"
_kcc_x86_s_cqto:     .asciz "\tcqto\n"
_kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx:  .asciz "\txorq\t%rdx, %rdx\n"
_kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx: .asciz "\tidivq\t%rcx\n"
_kcc_x86_s_div_rcx:  .asciz "\tdivq\t%rcx\n"

// Register operands and common instruction tails.
_kcc_x86_s_r10:      .asciz "%r10"
_kcc_x86_s_r11:      .asciz "%r11"
_kcc_x86_s_rax:      .asciz "%rax"
_kcc_x86_s_rcx:      .asciz "%rcx"
_kcc_x86_s_rdx:      .asciz "%rdx"
_kcc_x86_s_r11_r10:  .asciz "%r11, %r10\n"   // binary op tail: dst = dst OP r11
_kcc_x86_s_r10_nl:   .asciz "%r10\n"          // unary op tail
_kcc_x86_s_cl_r10:   .asciz "%cl, %r10\n"     // shift-by-%cl tail
_kcc_x86_s_c_r10:    .asciz ", %r10\n"        // ", %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_c_r10d:   .asciz ", %r10d\n"       // ", %r10d\n"
// Memory operand tails for load (source (%r11)) and store (dest (%r11)).
_kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10: .asciz "(%r11), %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d:.asciz "(%r11), %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11: .asciz "%r10, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11:.asciz "%r10b, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11:.asciz "%r10w, (%r11)\n"
_kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11:.asciz "%r10d, (%r11)\n"
@}

\subsection{Vocabulary for Control Flow and Calls (Rung 3)}
\label{sec:x86-data-rung3}

Rung~three adds the comparison, branch, and call vocabulary.  Comparisons emit
a \texttt{cmp} at the operands' common width followed by a \texttt{set}\emph{cc}
into \verb|%al| and a \texttt{movzbl} that widens the boolean into
\verb|%r10|.  Branches are the great simplification of assembly emission: a
basic block becomes a named label \verb|.Lkccbb|$N$ (block ids are globally
unique), so an \texttt{IR\_BR} is a \texttt{jmp} to that label and the
assembler resolves forward and backward references itself---no patch vector.
Calls follow the System~V AMD64 ABI: the integer argument registers, in order,
are \verb|%rdi|, \verb|%rsi|, \verb|%rdx|, \verb|%rcx|, \verb|%r8|, \verb|%r9|
(collected in \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_argregs} for index-based lookup), the return
value comes back in \verb|%rax|, and a direct call names the callee with the
Mach-O leading underscore.

@d X86: data @{
// Comparison: cmp at width, set<cc> into %al, widen with movzbl.
_kcc_x86_s_cmpl:     .asciz "\tcmpl\t%r11d, %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cmpq:     .asciz "\tcmpq\t%r11, %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al:.asciz "\tmovzbl\t%al, %r10d\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sete:     .asciz "\tsete\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setne:    .asciz "\tsetne\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setl:     .asciz "\tsetl\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setle:    .asciz "\tsetle\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setg:     .asciz "\tsetg\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setge:    .asciz "\tsetge\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setb:     .asciz "\tsetb\t%al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_setbe:    .asciz "\tsetbe\t%al\n"

// Branches: named basic-block labels resolved by the assembler.
_kcc_x86_s_bb:       .asciz ".Lkccbb"        // label stem; a decimal id follows
_kcc_x86_s_label_nl: .asciz ":\n"
_kcc_x86_s_jmp:      .asciz "\tjmp\t"
_kcc_x86_s_jne:      .asciz "\tjne\t"
_kcc_x86_s_test_r10: .asciz "\ttestq\t%r10, %r10\n"

// Calls: System V AMD64 argument registers, call forms, and stack adjust.
_kcc_x86_s_rdi:      .asciz "%rdi"
_kcc_x86_s_rsi:      .asciz "%rsi"
_kcc_x86_s_r8:       .asciz "%r8"
_kcc_x86_s_r9:       .asciz "%r9"
_kcc_x86_s_call:     .asciz "\tcall\t"
_kcc_x86_s_call_r11: .asciz "\tcall\t*%r11\n"
_kcc_x86_s_zero_al:  .asciz "\tmovb\t$0, %al\n"
_kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp:  .asciz "\tsubq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_add_rsp:  .asciz "\taddq\t$"
_kcc_x86_s_rsp:      .asciz ", %rsp\n"
_kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp:.asciz "(%rsp)"
_kcc_x86_s_rip_r10:  .asciz "(%rip), %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_got_r10:  .asciz "@@GOTPCREL(%rip), %r10\n"

// Data emission: zeroed common storage for a file-scope global.
_kcc_x86_s_zerofill: .asciz "\t.zerofill __DATA,__common,_"
_kcc_x86_s_zf_a3:    .asciz ",3\n"      // ",<size>,3\n" alignment tail (8-byte)
_kcc_x86_s_comma1:   .asciz ","

// Refusal diagnostic for an opcode the back end does not yet implement.
_kcc_x86_s_unsup:    .asciz "kcc: --target=x86_64: unsupported operation (floating point, inline asm, or by-value aggregate not yet implemented for the x86-64 back end)\n"

// String literals: a private label per literal, its address via leaq, and the
// cstring section that holds the bytes.
_kcc_x86_s_strlbl:   .asciz ".Lx86str"      // label stem; a decimal id follows
_kcc_x86_s_cstrsect: .asciz ".section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals\n"
_kcc_x86_s_byte:     .asciz "\t.byte "
_kcc_x86_s_zero_nl:  .asciz "0\n"

// The argument-register lookup table holds pointers, so it lives in a
// relocatable data section, not the cstring pool (which admits no relocations).
.data
.p2align 3
_kcc_x86_argregs:
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rdi
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rsi
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rdx
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_rcx
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_r8
    .quad _kcc_x86_s_r9
@}

\subsection{Vocabulary for Floating Point (Rung 4)}
\label{sec:x86-data-fp}

Floating point is the fourth rung, and the one \kcc{} needs to compile itself
(its float-literal parser and its \texttt{const}-float narrowing both do real
FP).  The all-spill model carries a floating value in the same 8-byte slot an
integer uses---holding the IEEE-754 \emph{double} bit pattern, exactly as the
ARM64 back end does---so the only new machinery is the SSE instructions that
move a slot into an \texttt{\%xmm} register, operate, and write it back.
Two scratch SSE registers, \verb|%xmm0| and \verb|%xmm1|, mirror the integer
\verb|%r10|/\verb|%r11| pair.  A \texttt{float} in memory is the single-precision
narrowing of that double, so a width-code-8 load widens with \texttt{cvtss2sd}
and a width-code-8 store narrows with \texttt{cvtsd2ss}; every value the
selection carries between slots stays a double.

@d X86: data @{
.section __TEXT,__cstring,cstring_literals
// SSE moves and the scratch xmm operands.
_kcc_x86_s_movsd:    .asciz "\tmovsd\t"
_kcc_x86_s_movss:    .asciz "\tmovss\t"
_kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl:   .asciz ", %xmm0\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cx1_nl:   .asciz ", %xmm1\n"
_kcc_x86_s_x0_c:     .asciz "%xmm0, "
_kcc_x86_s_nl:       .asciz "\n"
// FP binary ops: dst(%xmm0) = %xmm0 OP %xmm1.
_kcc_x86_s_addsd:    .asciz "\taddsd\t"
_kcc_x86_s_subsd:    .asciz "\tsubsd\t"
_kcc_x86_s_mulsd:    .asciz "\tmulsd\t"
_kcc_x86_s_divsd:    .asciz "\tdivsd\t"
_kcc_x86_s_x1_x0:    .asciz "%xmm1, %xmm0\n"
// Conversions between the integer file, xmm, and single/double precision.
_kcc_x86_s_cvtsi2sd: .asciz "\tcvtsi2sdq\t%r10, %xmm0\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cvttsd2si:.asciz "\tcvttsd2si\t%xmm0, %r10\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cvtsd2ss: .asciz "\tcvtsd2ss\t%xmm0, %xmm0\n"
_kcc_x86_s_cvtss2sd: .asciz "\tcvtss2sd\t%xmm0, %xmm0\n"
// Single-precision memory operands (float load/store through %r11).
_kcc_x86_s_px11_x0:  .asciz "(%r11), %xmm0\n"
_kcc_x86_s_x0_px11:  .asciz "%xmm0, (%r11)\n"
@}

\section{Two Object-Format Flavours: Darwin and Linux}
\label{sec:x86-flavour}

The instruction \emph{selection} above is pure x86-64 and does not care which
operating system will host the program---but the surrounding assembly
\emph{directives} do.  Mach-O (Darwin) and ELF (Linux) disagree on three
things this back end emits: Mach-O prefixes every C symbol with an underscore
while ELF does not; their section directives are spelled differently
(\verb|.section __TEXT,__cstring| versus \verb|.section .rodata|,
\verb|.zerofill __DATA,__common| versus \verb|.comm|); and a reference to an
external symbol goes through the GOT on Darwin (a dylib import has no image
address) but resolves directly in a statically linked ELF executable.  A single
global, \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_os}, selects the flavour: \texttt{0} is Darwin (the
default, so the existing Mach-O path is untouched) and \texttt{1} is Linux.

The one pervasive difference---the underscore---is funnelled through a helper.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_sym} emits a symbol name preceded by an underscore only
under Darwin, so every label, \texttt{.globl}, and call target is written the
same way and the flavour is decided in one place.

@d X86: render @{
.text
// -- kcc_x86_sym(const char *name) — append a symbol: "_"+name on Darwin,
//    bare name on Linux/ELF.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_sym:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // name
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbnz x0, .Lx86sym_bare          // Linux: no underscore
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_us@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86sym_bare:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Text-Buffer Emission Helpers}
\label{sec:x86-helpers}

Selection is written in terms of a handful of primitives over the growable
text buffer (\S\ref{sec:emit-asm-buffer}): \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_str} appends a
NUL-terminated string, \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_ch} one byte, and
\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_dec} an unsigned decimal.  On top of those,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_disp} prints a frame displacement \verb|-D(%rbp)|, and
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_slot} prints the operand for virtual register~$V$, whose
displacement is $(V+1)\times 8$.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_load} emits
\verb|movq slot(V), reg| (a slot always holds a canonical 64-bit value, so a
plain \texttt{movq} reads any vreg), and \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_store} the inverse
\verb|movq reg, slot(V)|.  A register operand is passed by pointer to its name
string.

@d X86: render @{
.text
// -- kcc_x86_disp(uint64_t d) — append "-d(%rbp)" (a frame displacement).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_disp:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // d
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_minus@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_minus@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_slot(uint64_t v) — append the operand for vreg v: "-(v+1)*8(%rbp)".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_slot:
    add x0, x0, #1
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    b _kcc_x86_disp

// -- kcc_x86_load(uint64_t v, const char *reg) — emit "movq slot(v), reg".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_load:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    mov x20, x1                     // reg name
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_store(uint64_t v, const char *reg) — emit "movq reg, slot(v)".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_store:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    mov x20, x1                     // reg name
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Instruction Selection}
\label{sec:x86-sel}

Selection is a per-instruction dispatch: given one IR instruction, emit the
equivalent x86-64 and leave the result in the destination's slot.  The
opcode routes to a handler; anything rung~two does not yet implement (control
flow, floating point) falls through and emits nothing.  We build the handlers
bottom-up as a set of small helpers---one per instruction \emph{shape}---then
the dispatch that calls them.

\subsection{Binary, Unary, and Shift Shapes}
\label{sec:x86-alu}

The commutative and two-operand ALU instructions all share one shape: load
\texttt{SRC1} into \verb|%r10|, load \texttt{SRC2} into \verb|%r11|, emit the
mnemonic followed by \verb|%r11, %r10| (so \verb|%r10| becomes
\verb|%r10 OP %r11|), and store \verb|%r10| to \texttt{DEST}.  Subtraction is
non-commutative but the operand order is already correct
(\verb|subq %r11, %r10| is $\mathtt{src1}-\mathtt{src2}$).  The unary shape
(negate, complement) omits the second load.  The shift shape loads the count
into \verb|%rcx| because x86 variable shifts take their amount only in
\verb|%cl|.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_binop(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = src1 OP src2.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_binop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_unop(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = OP src1.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_unop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_shift(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — dst = src1 SHIFT src2 (%cl).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_shift:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cl_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cl_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_fpload(uint64_t v, const char *xmm_tail) — "movsd slot(v)<tail>".
//    xmm_tail is ", %xmm0\n" or ", %xmm1\n".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_fpload:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    mov x20, x1                     // tail
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movsd@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_fpstore(uint64_t v) — "movsd %xmm0, slot(v)".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_fpstore:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // v
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movsd@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_x0_c@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_x0_c@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_fpbinop(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem) — %xmm0 = src1 OP src2.
//    Loads both operands into %xmm0/%xmm1, applies the SSE op, stores %xmm0.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_fpbinop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpload
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx1_nl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx1_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpload
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_x1_x0@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_x1_x0@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    bl _kcc_x86_fpstore
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\subsection{Division and Remainder}
\label{sec:x86-div}

x86 division is the one integer operation with fixed register operands.  The
dividend goes in \verb|%rdx:%rax|; \texttt{idivq}/\texttt{divq} leaves the
quotient in \verb|%rax| and the remainder in \verb|%rdx|.  The helper loads
\texttt{SRC1} into \verb|%rax|, widens it into \verb|%rdx| (\texttt{cqto}
sign-extends for the signed forms, \verb|xorq %rdx, %rdx| zeroes it for the
unsigned ones), loads the divisor into \verb|%rcx|, divides, and stores either
\verb|%rax| (quotient, for \texttt{sdiv}/\texttt{udiv}) or \verb|%rdx|
(remainder, for \texttt{smod}/\texttt{umod}) to \texttt{DEST}.  Because the
operands were sign- or zero-extended when they were loaded from memory, the
full-width division agrees with C's trunc-toward-zero semantics on the narrow
type.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_divop(ir_instr *i, uint64_t is_signed, uint64_t want_rem)
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_divop:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // is_signed
    mov x21, x2                     // want_rem
    // %rax = src1
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // Widen the dividend into %rdx.
    cbz x20, .Lx86div_unsigned
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cqto@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cqto@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86div_divisor
.Lx86div_unsigned:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_xor_rdx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86div_divisor:
    // %rcx = src2
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // idivq / divq %rcx
    cbz x20, .Lx86div_unsigned_op
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_idiv_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86div_result
.Lx86div_unsigned_op:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_div_rcx@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_div_rcx@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86div_result:
    // Store %rax (quotient) or %rdx (remainder) to DEST.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    cbnz x21, .Lx86div_rem
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86div_store
.Lx86div_rem:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rdx@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rdx@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86div_store:
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\subsection{Width-Changing Conversions}
\label{sec:x86-conv}

The four conversions all read \texttt{SRC1} straight from its slot with a
single extending move---the low bytes of the canonical slot value are exactly
the narrow source---and store the widened result.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_extend} takes the mnemonic and the destination-register
tail (\verb|, %r10| for the 64-bit forms, \verb|, %r10d| for \texttt{movl},
which zero-extends into \verb|%r10|) and emits \verb|mnem slot(SRC1), reg|.
\texttt{IR\_SEXT} and \texttt{IR\_ZEXT} choose the mnemonic from their source
width in \texttt{SRC2} (\texttt{movsbq}/\texttt{movswq}/\texttt{movslq} signed,
\texttt{movzbq}/\texttt{movzwq}/\texttt{movl} unsigned); \texttt{IR\_TRUNC}
chooses from its \emph{target} width in \texttt{IR\_TYPE} and zero-extends the
surviving low bytes, matching the ARM64 mask-and-copy; \texttt{IR\_BITCAST} is
a plain \texttt{movq} copy.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_extend(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *tail)
//    Emit "mnem slot(SRC1)tail" then store %r10 to DEST.  `tail` supplies the
//    ", %r10\n" (or ", %r10d\n") destination-register text.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_extend:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // tail
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_x86_slot
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_conv(ir_instr *i, uint64_t width, uint64_t is_signed)
//    Pick the extending mnemonic/tail for a `width`-byte source and emit it.
//    is_signed selects movs* vs movz*/movl; width 8 or 0 is a plain movq copy.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_conv:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x1                     // width
    mov x20, x2                     // is_signed
    // x0 already holds instr; stash it so the dispatch below can reload.
    str x0, [sp, #-16]!            // save instr (16-byte aligned push)
    cmp x19, #1
    b.eq .Lx86conv_b
    cmp x19, #2
    b.eq .Lx86conv_w
    cmp x19, #4
    b.eq .Lx86conv_l
    // Default (8 or unknown): movq copy, no extension.
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_b:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_bz
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86conv_b_go
.Lx86conv_bz:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86conv_b_go:
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_w:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_wz
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86conv_w_go
.Lx86conv_wz:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86conv_w_go:
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_l:
    ldr x0, [sp], #16
    cbz x20, .Lx86conv_lz
    // Signed 32→64: movslq slot, %r10.
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
    b .Lx86conv_done
.Lx86conv_lz:
    // Unsigned 32→64: movl slot, %r10d (implicitly zero-extends into %r10).
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10d@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10d@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_extend
.Lx86conv_done:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\subsection{Memory Operations}
\label{sec:x86-mem}

\texttt{IR\_LOAD} dereferences a pointer with an access-size override in
\texttt{SRC2} (the code $1/2/4$ unsigned, $5/6/7$ signed, $0$ eight-byte;
$8$ is a single-precision float, deferred to a later rung).  The helper loads
the pointer into \verb|%r11|, emits the width-appropriate move from
\verb|(%r11)| into \verb|%r10| (or \verb|%r10d| for the zero-extending
32-bit \texttt{movl}), and stores \verb|%r10| to \texttt{DEST}.
\texttt{IR\_STORE} is the inverse with its size override in \texttt{IR\_DEST}
($0/1/2/4$): load the value into \verb|%r10| and the pointer into \verb|%r11|,
then \verb|mov %r10x, (%r11)| at the right width.  \texttt{IR\_GEP} (get
element pointer) is the address arithmetic that array subscripts and struct
member accesses lower to: its second operand is already a \emph{byte} offset
(the IR builder pre-scales a subscript by the element size), so the address is
just \texttt{SRC1}${}+{}$\texttt{SRC2}---the very shape of a two-operand ALU
add, and so it reuses \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_binop} with \texttt{addq}.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_memload(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *tail)
//    Emit "mov* (%r11), reg" for a pointer already implied by SRC1, storing to
//    DEST.  Loads the pointer into %r11 first.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_memload:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // tail  "(%r11), %r10\n" etc.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_memstore(ir_instr *i, const char *mnem, const char *valtail)
//    Emit "mov* reg, (%r11)".  Loads value into %r10, pointer into %r11.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_memstore:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0
    mov x20, x1                     // mnemonic
    mov x21, x2                     // valtail  "%r10, (%r11)\n" etc.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // value
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // pointer
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\subsection{Comparison, Label, and Call Helpers (Rung 3)}
\label{sec:x86-rung3-helpers}

Three more shape helpers cover control flow.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_cmp} emits a
comparison: it loads both operands, compares them at the width recorded in the
instruction's type (32-bit \texttt{cmpl} for an \texttt{int}-or-narrower common
type, else 64-bit \texttt{cmpq}), materialises the boolean with the caller's
chosen \texttt{set}\emph{cc} into \verb|%al|, widens it into \verb|%r10| with
\texttt{movzbl}, and stores it.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_bblabel} appends a
basic-block label reference \verb|.Lkccbb|$N$ (used both to define a block's
label and to name a branch target).  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_marshal} realises the
System~V argument-passing rules for the integer class: it reads the argument
array (\texttt{[count\,|\,fsi{<}{<}32, vreg0, class0, \ldots]}), places
arguments seven and beyond into a freshly subtracted stack region at
\verb|(k-6)*8(%rsp)|, loads the first six into \verb|%rdi|--\verb|%r9|, and
returns the \texttt{stack\_adj} the caller adds back after the call.
Floating-point and by-value aggregate arguments are a later rung; rung~three
treats every argument as integer/pointer.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_cmp(ir_instr *i, const char *setcc) — dst = (src1 CMP src2) ? 1:0.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_cmp:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    mov x20, x1                     // set<cc> string
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // Compare width: <=4-byte common type → 32-bit cmpl, else 64-bit cmpq.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86cmp_q
    ldr x0, [x0, #TY_SIZE]
    cmp x0, #8
    b.eq .Lx86cmp_q
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpl@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86cmp_emit
.Lx86cmp_q:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cmpq@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86cmp_emit:
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20                     // set<cc> %al
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movzbl_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_bblabel(uint64_t bb_id) — append ".Lkccbb<id>".
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_bblabel:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_bb@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_bb@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_marshal(uint64_t *args, uint64_t count) → stack_adj.
//    Loads integer/pointer args into %rdi..%r9 and args 7+ onto the stack.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_marshal:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // args array
    mov x20, x1                     // count
    mov x22, #0                     // stack_adj (return value)
    cbz x20, .Lx86mar_done

    // Stack phase: if count > 6, reserve align16((count-6)*8) and store the
    // overflow args (index 6..count-1) at (k-6)*8(%rsp).
    cmp x20, #6
    b.le .Lx86mar_regs
    sub x0, x20, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    add x0, x0, #15
    and x22, x0, #~15              // stack_adj
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x21, #6                     // k = 6
.Lx86mar_stack_loop:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86mar_regs
    // movq slot(vreg_k), %r10
    lsl x0, x21, #1
    add x0, x0, #1                 // 1 + 2k → vreg field
    ldr x0, [x19, x0, lsl #3]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    // movq %r10, (k-6)*8(%rsp)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    sub x0, x21, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3                // (k-6)*8
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_paren_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86mar_stack_loop

.Lx86mar_regs:
    // Register phase: args 0..min(count,6)-1 into %rdi..%r9.
    mov x21, #0
.Lx86mar_reg_loop:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86mar_done
    cmp x21, #6
    b.ge .Lx86mar_done
    lsl x0, x21, #1
    add x0, x0, #1                 // 1 + 2k → vreg field
    ldr x0, [x19, x0, lsl #3]      // vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1, x21, lsl #3]      // argreg[k] string ptr
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86mar_reg_loop

.Lx86mar_done:
    mov x0, x22                     // stack_adj
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_call_args(uint64_t *args) → stack_adj.  Reads the count from the
//    argument array header and marshals; a null array is a zero-argument call.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_call_args:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
    mov x29, sp
    cbz x0, .Lx86ca_none
    ldr x1, [x0]                    // word0 = count | (fsi << 32)
    and x1, x1, #0xffffffff         // count
    bl _kcc_x86_marshal             // marshal(args=x0, count=x1) → x0 = stack_adj
    b .Lx86ca_ret
.Lx86ca_none:
    mov x0, #0
.Lx86ca_ret:
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
    ret
@}

\subsection{The Dispatch}
\label{sec:x86-dispatch}

With the shape helpers in hand, \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_sel} is a flat opcode
switch.  \texttt{IR\_CONST} materialises its immediate with \texttt{movabsq}
(the one x86 move that takes a full 64-bit immediate) into \verb|%r10| and
stores it.  \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} computes the \emph{address} of its storage---%
\verb|-(vreg_n + offset + size)*8(%rbp)|, where the pre-scan left the running
slot offset in \texttt{SRC2} and \texttt{SRC1} is the object's slot count---%
with a \texttt{leaq} and stores that pointer.  \texttt{IR\_RET} loads the
returned vreg into \verb|%rax| (the low 32 bits are the \texttt{int} result
the process exit status reads) and falls into the \texttt{leave}/\texttt{retq}
epilogue; a \texttt{void} return skips straight to it.  Everything else routes
to a shape helper.

An opcode the back end has \emph{not} yet learned---a floating-point operation,
inline assembly, a by-value aggregate---does not fall through silently.  A
genuine \texttt{IR\_NOP} emits nothing and is skipped, but any other unrecognised
opcode makes selection print a diagnostic and exit non-zero.  This is a
deliberate choice: emitting nothing for, say, an \texttt{IR\_FMUL} would leave
its destination slot holding garbage and \emph{miscompile} the program with no
warning---the worst possible failure.  Refusing cleanly instead keeps a
program that uses an unimplemented feature (a \texttt{double}-heavy interpreter
like Lua) an honest compile-time error rather than a wrong answer at run time,
matching the front end's treatment of VLAs and \texttt{\_Complex}.  As each
later rung teaches the back end a new opcode, the set that trips the refusal
shrinks.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_sel(ir_instr *i) — select x86-64 for one IR instruction.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_sel:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // instr
    ldr x22, [x19, #IR_OP]

    cmp x22, #IR_CONST
    b.eq .Lx86sel_const
    cmp x22, #IR_RET
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ret
    cmp x22, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.eq .Lx86sel_alloca
    cmp x22, #IR_LOAD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load
    cmp x22, #IR_STORE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store

    // Two-operand ALU: route by opcode to a mnemonic, then kcc_x86_binop.
    // IR_GEP is address arithmetic (base + byte offset), i.e. a plain addq.
    cmp x22, #IR_ADD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_add
    cmp x22, #IR_GEP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_add
    cmp x22, #IR_SUB
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sub
    cmp x22, #IR_MUL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_mul
    cmp x22, #IR_AND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_and
    cmp x22, #IR_OR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_or
    cmp x22, #IR_XOR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_xor
    cmp x22, #IR_NEG
    b.eq .Lx86sel_neg
    cmp x22, #IR_NOT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_not
    cmp x22, #IR_SHL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_shl
    cmp x22, #IR_LSHR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_lshr
    cmp x22, #IR_ASHR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ashr
    cmp x22, #IR_SDIV
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sdiv
    cmp x22, #IR_UDIV
    b.eq .Lx86sel_udiv
    cmp x22, #IR_SMOD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_smod
    cmp x22, #IR_UMOD
    b.eq .Lx86sel_umod
    cmp x22, #IR_SEXT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sext
    cmp x22, #IR_ZEXT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_zext
    cmp x22, #IR_TRUNC
    b.eq .Lx86sel_trunc
    cmp x22, #IR_BITCAST
    b.eq .Lx86sel_bitcast

    // Floating-point conversions (rung 4).  UITOFP shares the signed cvtsi2sd
    // path: kcc's own source only ever converts signed integers to floating.
    cmp x22, #IR_SITOFP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sitofp
    cmp x22, #IR_UITOFP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_sitofp
    cmp x22, #IR_FPTOSI
    b.eq .Lx86sel_fptosi
    cmp x22, #IR_FPROUND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_fpround

    // Comparisons (rung 3): cmp + set<cc> + movzbl.
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_EQ
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpeq
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_NE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpne
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SLT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpslt
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SLE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsle
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SGT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsgt
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_SGE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpsge
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_ULT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpult
    cmp x22, #IR_CMP_ULE
    b.eq .Lx86sel_cmpule

    // Control flow and calls (rung 3).
    cmp x22, #IR_BR
    b.eq .Lx86sel_br
    cmp x22, #IR_BR_COND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_brcond
    cmp x22, #IR_CALL
    b.eq .Lx86sel_call
    cmp x22, #IR_CALL_IND
    b.eq .Lx86sel_callind
    cmp x22, #IR_GLOBAL_REF
    b.eq .Lx86sel_globalref
    cmp x22, #IR_STRING_LIT
    b.eq .Lx86sel_strlit

    // A genuine no-op emits nothing and is safe to skip.
    cmp x22, #IR_NOP
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done

    // Any other opcode is a feature this back end does not yet implement
    // (floating point, inline asm, by-value aggregates, ...).  Silently
    // skipping it would emit a value-less slot and miscompile the program, so
    // refuse cleanly instead---the same discipline the front end applies to
    // VLAs and _Complex.  This is what keeps a double-using program (Lua) an
    // honest compile-time error rather than a wrong answer at run time.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_unsup@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_unsup@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_io_err
    bl _kcc_io_flush
    mov x0, #1
    bl _kcc_sys_exit

.Lx86sel_const:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movabsq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movabsq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_alloca:
    // D = (vreg_n + offset + size) * 8.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_SRC2]        // offset (slots, from pre-scan)
    ldr x21, [x19, #IR_SRC1]        // size (slots)
    cbnz x21, .Lx86sel_alloca_sz
    mov x21, #1
.Lx86sel_alloca_sz:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    add x0, x0, x20
    add x0, x0, x21
    lsl x20, x0, #3                 // D (bytes)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_x86_disp
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_c_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_ret:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_ret_epi        // null type → void
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_VOID
    b.eq .Lx86sel_ret_epi
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
.Lx86sel_ret_epi:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leave@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leave@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_retq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_retq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_load:
    // Access-size override in SRC2 selects the extending move.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    cmp x20, #1
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u8
    cmp x20, #2
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u16
    cmp x20, #4
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_u32
    cmp x20, #5
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s8
    cmp x20, #6
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s16
    cmp x20, #7
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_s32
    cmp x20, #8
    b.eq .Lx86sel_load_f32          // single-precision float: movss + widen
    // Default: 64-bit movq.
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzbq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movzwq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_u32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10d@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movsbq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movswq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_s32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movslq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_pr11_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memload
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_store:
    // Access-size override in DEST selects the store width.
    ldr x20, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    cmp x20, #1
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_8
    cmp x20, #2
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_16
    cmp x20, #4
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_32
    cmp x20, #8
    b.eq .Lx86sel_store_f32         // single-precision float: narrow + movss
    // Default: 64-bit movq.
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_8:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movb@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movb@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10b_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_16:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movw@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movw@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10w_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_32:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_movl@@PAGEOFF
    adrp x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11@@PAGE
    add  x2, x2, _kcc_x86_s_r10d_pr11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_memstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_load_f32:
    // float in memory → double in the slot: load ptr into %r11, movss the
    // single, widen with cvtss2sd, store the double.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movss@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movss@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_px11_x0@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_px11_x0@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtss2sd@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtss2sd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    bl _kcc_x86_fpstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_store_f32:
    // double in the slot → float in memory: load the double into %xmm0, narrow
    // with cvtsd2ss, load the address into %r11, movss the single.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpload
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsd2ss@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsd2ss@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movss@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movss@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_x0_px11@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_x0_px11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Two-operand ALU tails: load mnemonic, call the binop shape. ----
.Lx86sel_add:
    // A floating ADD/SUB/MUL/DIV (typed float/double) uses the SSE unit; the
    // shared IR_GEP that also lands here is integer address arithmetic, so its
    // int type keeps it on the integer path.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_add_i
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_addsd@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_addsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpbinop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_add_i:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_addq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_addq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sub:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_sub_i
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_subsd@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_subsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpbinop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sub_i:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_subq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_subq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_mul:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_mul_i
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_mulsd@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_mulsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpbinop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_mul_i:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_imulq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_imulq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_and:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_andq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_andq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_or:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_orq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_orq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_xor:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_xorq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_xorq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_binop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_neg:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_negq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_negq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_unop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_not:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_notq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_notq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_unop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_shl:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_shlq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_shlq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_lshr:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_shrq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_shrq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_ashr:
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_sarq@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_sarq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_shift
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sdiv:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    bl _kcc_ir_type_is_fp
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_sdiv_i
    mov x0, x19
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_divsd@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_divsd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpbinop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sdiv_i:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #1                      // signed
    mov x2, #0                      // quotient
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Floating-point conversions (rung 4). ----
.Lx86sel_sitofp:
    // int slot → double: cvtsi2sdq %r10, %xmm0 ; movsd %xmm0, slot(dest).
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsi2sd@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsi2sd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    bl _kcc_x86_fpstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_fptosi:
    // double slot → int: movsd slot(src),%xmm0 ; cvttsd2si %xmm0,%r10 ; store.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpload
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvttsd2si@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvttsd2si@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_fpround:
    // round a double to single precision, keeping it in double format:
    // movsd slot(src),%xmm0 ; cvtsd2ss ; cvtss2sd ; movsd %xmm0, slot(dest).
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_cx0_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_fpload
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsd2ss@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtsd2ss@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtss2sd@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cvtss2sd@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    bl _kcc_x86_fpstore
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_udiv:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #0
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_smod:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #1
    mov x2, #1                      // remainder
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_umod:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #0
    mov x2, #1
    bl _kcc_x86_divop
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_sext:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // source width
    mov x2, #1                      // signed
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_zext:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // source width
    mov x2, #0                      // unsigned
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_trunc:
    mov x0, x19
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_TYPE]         // target type → width
    cbz x1, .Lx86sel_trunc_copy
    ldr x1, [x1, #TY_SIZE]
    mov x2, #0                      // truncation keeps low bytes, zero-extended
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_trunc_copy:
    mov x1, #8                      // unknown width → plain copy
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done
.Lx86sel_bitcast:
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, #8                      // plain movq copy
    mov x2, #0
    bl _kcc_x86_conv
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Comparisons: load the set<cc> mnemonic, call the cmp shape. ----
.Lx86sel_cmpeq:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_sete@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_sete@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpne:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setne@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setne@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpslt:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setl@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setl@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsle:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setle@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setle@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsgt:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setg@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setg@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpsge:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setge@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setge@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpult:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setb@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setb@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86sel_cmp_go
.Lx86sel_cmpule:
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_setbe@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_setbe@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86sel_cmp_go:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_cmp
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Unconditional branch: jmp .Lkccbb<target>. ----
.Lx86sel_br:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // target BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Conditional branch: test cond, jne true, jmp false. ----
.Lx86sel_brcond:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]         // condition vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_test_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_test_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jne@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jne@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // true BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_jmp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // false BB id
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Global reference: the symbol's address into %r10, stored to dest. ----
.Lx86sel_globalref:
    // Darwin: movq _<name>@@GOTPCREL(%rip), %r10 --- the GOT form resolves
    // uniformly for a locally defined symbol and for a dylib import (like puts),
    // which has no fixed image address, exactly the ARM64 GOTPAGE discipline.
    // Linux/ELF (static): leaq <name>(%rip), %r10 --- a statically linked
    // executable resolves every symbol to a real address at link time, so the
    // direct RIP form suffices and no GOT slot is needed.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbnz x0, .Lx86gref_elf
    // Darwin GOT form.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // symbol name
    bl _kcc_x86_sym                 // "_"+name on Darwin
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_got_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_got_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86gref_store
.Lx86gref_elf:
    // Linux direct form: leaq <name>(%rip), %r10.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]
    bl _kcc_x86_sym                 // bare name on Linux
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86gref_store:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- String literal: record (bytes,len), leaq .Lx86str<id>(%rip), %r10. ----
.Lx86sel_strlit:
    // id = current count of the string table; record {SRC1, SRC2}.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    ldr x20, [x0, #VEC_COUNT]       // id = current entry count
    ldr x1, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // data ptr
    ldr x2, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // length
    stp x1, x2, [sp, #-16]!         // build the {ptr, len} entry
    mov x1, sp
    bl _kcc_vec_push                // push(strs, &entry)
    add sp, sp, #16
    // leaq .Lx86str<id>(%rip), %r10
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_leaq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20                     // id
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rip_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

// ---- Direct call: marshal args, call _<name>, store %rax. ----
.Lx86sel_call:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // args array (0 if none)
    bl _kcc_x86_call_args           // loads args into ABI regs/stack
    mov x20, x0                     // x20 = stack_adj (survives the call sequence)
    // movb $0, %al   (variadic safety: no FP args in rung 3)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // call <name>  (underscore-prefixed on Darwin via kcc_x86_sym)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_call@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_call@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // callee name
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86sel_call_tail

// ---- Indirect call: marshal args, call *%r11, store %rax. ----
.Lx86sel_callind:
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC2]         // args array
    bl _kcc_x86_call_args           // loads args into ABI regs/stack
    mov x20, x0                     // x20 = stack_adj
    // Load the function pointer into %r11 (arg regs already set; r11 is free).
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_SRC1]         // fn-ptr vreg
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_load
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_al@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_call_r11@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_call_r11@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // fall through to the shared return/stack-restore tail

.Lx86sel_call_tail:
    // Restore the stack if arguments overflowed onto it (x20 = stack_adj).
    cbz x20, .Lx86sel_call_ret
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_add_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_add_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rsp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86sel_call_ret:
    // Store %rax into the result vreg unless the call is void.
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_TYPE]
    cbz x0, .Lx86sel_done           // no type → nothing to home
    ldr x1, [x0, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x1, #TY_VOID
    b.eq .Lx86sel_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #IR_DEST]
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_rax@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sel_done

.Lx86sel_done:
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\section{The Frame: Pre-scan, Prologue, Epilogue}
\label{sec:x86-frame}

Before a function's instructions can be selected, its frame must be sized.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_prescan} walks every basic block and, for each
\texttt{IR\_ALLOCA}, records the running slot offset in \texttt{SRC2} and
advances by the alloca's slot count (\texttt{SRC1}, defaulting to one).  It
returns the total number of alloca slots.  The vreg value slots and the alloca
storage together fix the frame size; the prologue emits the standard
\verb|pushq %rbp| / \verb|movq %rsp, %rbp| and, when the frame is non-empty,
one \verb|subq $frame, %rsp| (rounded up to a 16-byte multiple so the stack
stays aligned).  The \texttt{leave} in every \texttt{IR\_RET} epilogue undoes
all of it in one instruction, so no per-function epilogue state is needed.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_prescan(ir_func *f) → total alloca slots; sets each alloca's SRC2.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_prescan:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    mov x19, x0                     // func
    mov x20, #0                     // running offset (slots)
    ldr x21, [x19, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    cbz x21, .Lx86ps_done
    ldr x22, [x21, #VEC_COUNT]
    mov x23, #0                     // bb index
.Lx86ps_bb:
    cmp x23, x22
    b.ge .Lx86ps_done
    mov x0, x21
    mov x1, x23
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x24, [x0]                   // BB ptr
    ldr x24, [x24, #BB_FIRST]
.Lx86ps_instr:
    cbz x24, .Lx86ps_next_bb
    ldr x0, [x24, #IR_OP]
    cmp x0, #IR_ALLOCA
    b.ne .Lx86ps_skip
    str x20, [x24, #IR_SRC2]        // record this alloca's offset
    ldr x0, [x24, #IR_SRC1]         // slot count
    cbnz x0, .Lx86ps_add
    mov x0, #1
.Lx86ps_add:
    add x20, x20, x0
.Lx86ps_skip:
    ldr x24, [x24, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lx86ps_instr
.Lx86ps_next_bb:
    add x23, x23, #1
    b .Lx86ps_bb
.Lx86ps_done:
    mov x0, x20
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret
@}

\section{Spilling Incoming Parameters}
\label{sec:x86-params}

On the callee side, the System~V ABI delivers the first six integer parameters
in \verb|%rdi|--\verb|%r9| and the rest on the caller's stack.  Because the IR
numbers a function's parameters as its first vregs (\texttt{\%0}, \texttt{\%1},
\ldots), the prologue copies each incoming register into its parameter's vreg
slot, after which a parameter is indistinguishable from any other value in the
all-spill model.  A parameter past the sixth is read from the caller's stack
image at \verb|16 + (k-6)*8(%rbp)| (the saved \verb|%rbp| and return address
occupy the first sixteen bytes above \verb|%rbp|).  As with the argument
marshalling, rung~three assumes every parameter is of the integer class;
floating-point and by-value aggregate parameters are a later rung.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_spill_params(ir_func *f) — copy incoming args into vreg slots.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_spill_params:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldr x19, [x0, #IRF_PARAMS]      // param count
    mov x20, #0                     // k
.Lx86sp_loop:
    cmp x20, x19
    b.ge .Lx86sp_done
    cmp x20, #6
    b.ge .Lx86sp_stack
    // movq %argreg[k], slot(k)
    mov x0, x20                     // vreg = k
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_argregs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x1, [x1, x20, lsl #3]       // argreg[k] string ptr
    bl _kcc_x86_store
    b .Lx86sp_next
.Lx86sp_stack:
    // movq 16+(k-6)*8(%rbp), %r10   (positive displacement: caller stack image)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_movq@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    sub x0, x20, #6
    lsl x0, x0, #3
    add x0, x0, #16                 // 16 + (k-6)*8
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    // store %r10 → slot(k)
    mov x0, x20
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_s_r10@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_store
.Lx86sp_next:
    add x20, x20, #1
    b .Lx86sp_loop
.Lx86sp_done:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Walking the Module}
\label{sec:x86-walk}

The top-level walk mirrors the IR dumper (\S\ref{sec:ir-dump}): iterate the
function list \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_funcs}, and for each function emit its symbol
declaration and label, publish its vreg count, run the pre-scan, emit the
prologue, then stream its instructions through selection.  A
non-\texttt{static} function gets a \texttt{.globl}; the label and declaration
route the name through \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_sym}, so it carries the Mach-O
leading underscore under Darwin and none under Linux/ELF.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_render_text() — walk the IR, emit x86-64 assembly to the buffer.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_render_text:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-96]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    str x27, [sp, #80]

    // Section directive.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_text@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_text@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    adrp x0, _kcc_ir_funcs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_ir_funcs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86rt_done
    ldr x20, [x19, #VEC_COUNT]      // func count
    mov x21, #0                     // func index

.Lx86rt_func:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86rt_done
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x21
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x22, [x0]                   // func ptr

    // .globl <name>   (unless static, or the synthesised module-init function).
    // "kcc.module_init" must have internal linkage: every translation unit with
    // a file-scope initialiser emits one, and exporting them would collide at
    // link time.  The ARM64 code generator special-cases the same name.
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_STATIC]
    cbnz x0, .Lx86rt_label
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_NAME]
    adrp x1, _kcc_str_modinit@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_str_modinit@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_strcmp
    cbz x0, .Lx86rt_label          // name == "kcc.module_init" → no .globl
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_NAME]
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    mov x0, #10                     // '\n'
    bl _kcc_as_ch

.Lx86rt_label:
    // <name>:  (underscore-prefixed on Darwin)
    ldr x0, [x22, #IRF_NAME]
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    // Publish the vreg count (alloca addressing reads it) and pre-scan allocas.
    ldr x26, [x22, #IRF_VREG_N]     // vreg count
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_vreg_n@@PAGEOFF
    str x26, [x0]
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_x86_prescan             // x0 = total alloca slots

    // frame_bytes = align16((vreg_count + alloca_slots) * 8).
    add x0, x0, x26
    lsl x0, x0, #3                  // bytes
    add x0, x0, #15
    and x0, x0, #~15
    mov x27, x0                     // frame_bytes

    // Prologue: pushq %rbp ; movq %rsp, %rbp ; subq $frame, %rsp.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_push_rbp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_push_rbp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_mov_sp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_mov_sp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    cbz x27, .Lx86rt_body
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_sp@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sub_sp@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_sp_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_sp_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

.Lx86rt_body:
    // Spill incoming parameters into their vreg slots (SysV integer class).
    mov x0, x22
    bl _kcc_x86_spill_params

    // Walk basic blocks.
    ldr x23, [x22, #IRF_BLOCKS]
    cbz x23, .Lx86rt_next_func
    ldr x24, [x23, #VEC_COUNT]      // block count
    mov x25, #0                     // block index

.Lx86rt_bb:
    cmp x25, x24
    b.ge .Lx86rt_next_func
    mov x0, x23
    mov x1, x25
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x27, [x0]                   // BB ptr
    // Emit the block's label ".Lkccbb<id>:" so branches can target it.
    ldr x0, [x27, #BB_ID]
    bl _kcc_x86_bblabel
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    ldr x27, [x27, #BB_FIRST]       // first instruction

.Lx86rt_instr:
    cbz x27, .Lx86rt_next_bb
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_x86_sel
    ldr x27, [x27, #IR_NEXT]
    b .Lx86rt_instr

.Lx86rt_next_bb:
    add x25, x25, #1
    b .Lx86rt_bb

.Lx86rt_next_func:
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86rt_func

.Lx86rt_done:
    ldr x27, [sp, #80]
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #96
    ret
@}

\section{Emitting Global Variable Storage}
\label{sec:x86-data-emit}

Functions are only half of a translation unit; the other half is its
file-scope data.  \kcc{} emits a \emph{mutable} global's initialiser as
runtime stores (they were streamed into \texttt{main} above, exactly like any
other assignment), so the storage itself only has to be reserved and
zeroed---an initialised store then overwrites the zero with the real value.
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_data} walks the AST translation unit
(\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_tu}, published by the driver) and, for each defining
variable declaration, emits a \verb|.zerofill __DATA,__common| reservation at
the declared type's size (floored at eight bytes and rounded to a whole
eight-byte slot, matching the ARM64 pre-registration).  An external global
(\texttt{extern}) is a declaration, not a definition, and is skipped; a
\texttt{static} file-scope global gets no \texttt{.globl} (internal linkage);
everything else is exported so other units can link against it.

A \texttt{const} global whose initialiser is a fixed byte image takes a
different path.  \kcc{} realises those as read-only data with their actual
bytes and emits \emph{no} runtime store, so zeroed storage would read as zero
rather than the initialiser.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_rodata\_global}
(\S\ref{sec:x86-rodata}) handles the \emph{scalar and array-of-scalar} cases by
emitting the initialiser as \texttt{.byte}/\texttt{.short}/\texttt{.long}/%
\texttt{.quad} value directives; the aggregate cases it does not yet
lay out (a \texttt{const} struct, a 1-D array of structs, or a \texttt{const}
pointer array) decline and stay undefined---a clean link error, not a wrong
value---as does a block-scoped \texttt{static} local, whose mangled
\texttt{sl\_}\meta{node} storage would need a walk into function bodies.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_zerofill(const char *name, uint64_t size, uint64_t is_static)
//    Emit zeroed common storage.  Darwin: .zerofill __DATA,__common,_<name>,size,3.
//    Linux/ELF: .comm <name>,size,8.  A non-static symbol is exported (.globl).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_zerofill:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // name
    mov x20, x1                     // size
    mov x21, x2                     // is_static
    // .globl <name>  unless static (internal linkage)
    cbnz x21, .Lx86zf_storage
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
.Lx86zf_storage:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbnz x0, .Lx86zf_comm
    // Darwin .zerofill: the directive string already carries the "_" prefix.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerofill@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerofill@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zf_a3@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zf_a3@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    b .Lx86zf_done
.Lx86zf_comm:
    // Linux .comm <name>,<size>,8
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comm@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comm@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_str                  // bare name (ELF: no underscore)
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comm_a8@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comm_a8@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86zf_done:
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_emit_data() — emit .zerofill storage for file-scope globals.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_data:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86ed_done
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F0]         // first top-level declaration
.Lx86ed_loop:
    cbz x19, .Lx86ed_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x0, #AST_VAR_DECL
    b.ne .Lx86ed_next
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_F1]         // name
    cbz x20, .Lx86ed_next
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // storage class
    cmp x0, #2                      // extern → declaration, no storage
    b.eq .Lx86ed_next
    cmp x0, #1                      // typedef → no storage
    b.eq .Lx86ed_next
    mov x21, x0                     // x21 = storage class (3 = static)
    // A const global that becomes a read-only byte image: emit its bytes.
    // The scalar / array-of-scalar cases are laid out here; the aggregate
    // cases decline (return 0) and fall through to stay undefined (deferred).
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_global_const_rodata
    cbz x0, .Lx86ed_chk_ptrarr
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x20                     // source name
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_rodata_global  // 1 = emitted, 0 = declined (aggregate)
    b .Lx86ed_next                  // either way, done with this decl
.Lx86ed_chk_ptrarr:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_global_const_ptrarr
    cbnz x0, .Lx86ed_next           // const pointer array: deferred
    // Size = round_up_8(max(8, type_layout(resolve(AST_F0)))).
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    cbz x0, .Lx86ed_next
    bl _kcc_type_layout
    mov x22, x0
    cmp x22, #8
    b.ge .Lx86ed_round
    mov x22, #8
.Lx86ed_round:
    add x22, x22, #7
    and x22, x22, #~7               // round up to a whole 8-byte slot

    // Emit zeroed common storage: name = x20, size = x22, static? = (x21 == 3).
    mov x0, x20
    mov x1, x22
    cmp x21, #3
    cset x2, eq
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_zerofill
.Lx86ed_next:
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F4]         // next sibling
    b .Lx86ed_loop
.Lx86ed_done:
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret
@}

\section{Static Locals}
\label{sec:x86-statics}

A block-scoped \texttt{static} local has static storage duration but is
declared inside a function body, so the top-level walk of
\S\ref{sec:x86-data-emit} never sees it.  \kcc{} hoists each such local to a
file-level global under a mangled \texttt{sl\_}\meta{node} name, and every
reference lowers to an \texttt{IR\_GLOBAL\_REF} to that name---so the storage
must be emitted or the symbol is undefined at link.  \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_statics}
recovers them by walking every function body exactly as the ARM64
pre-registration's second pass does (\S\ref{sec:preregister-globals}):
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_prereg\_stmt} descends compound blocks, the arms of
\texttt{if}/\texttt{switch}/loops, and \texttt{case}/\texttt{label}/%
\texttt{default} bodies, and for each \texttt{static} declaration it finds it
emits storage under the same mangled name \texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_static\_name}
produced during IR construction.  The split is identical to the file-scope
case: a \texttt{const} static with a scalar or array-of-scalar initialiser
becomes read-only data, and a mutable static becomes zeroed common storage that
its runtime initialiser fills---both with internal linkage (no \texttt{.globl}).

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_one_static(node *decl) — emit storage for one static local.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_one_static:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-48]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    str x21, [sp, #32]
    mov x19, x0                     // decl
    // Mangled name (matches the sl_<node> the IR_GLOBAL_REF uses).
    bl _kcc_ir_static_name
    mov x20, x0                     // name
    // const scalar/array-of-scalar → read-only data; else zeroed storage.
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_global_const_rodata
    cbz x0, .Lx86os_zero
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x20
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_rodata_global  // emits, or declines (aggregate → undefined)
    b .Lx86os_done
.Lx86os_zero:
    // Mutable static: size = round_up_8(max(8, layout)); zeroed, internal.
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    cbz x0, .Lx86os_done
    bl _kcc_type_layout
    mov x21, x0
    cmp x21, #8
    b.ge .Lx86os_round
    mov x21, #8
.Lx86os_round:
    add x21, x21, #7
    and x21, x21, #~7
    mov x0, x20                     // name
    mov x1, x21                     // size
    mov x2, #1                      // is_static (internal linkage)
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_zerofill
.Lx86os_done:
    ldr x21, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #48
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_prereg_stmt(node *stmt) — walk a statement (and its sibling chain),
//    emitting storage for every static local at any block depth.  Mirrors the
//    ARM64 pre-registration walker field for field.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_prereg_stmt:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // stmt cursor
.Lx86ps2_loop:
    cbz x19, .Lx86ps2_ret
    ldr x20, [x19, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x20, #AST_VAR_DECL
    b.ne .Lx86ps2_notvar
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // storage class (3 = static)
    cmp x0, #3
    b.ne .Lx86ps2_next
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_one_static
    b .Lx86ps2_next
.Lx86ps2_notvar:
    cmp x20, #AST_COMPOUND
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f0
    cmp x20, #AST_IF_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_if
    cmp x20, #AST_SWITCH_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f1
    cmp x20, #AST_WHILE_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f1
    cmp x20, #AST_DO_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f0
    cmp x20, #AST_FOR_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_for
    cmp x20, #AST_CASE_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f1
    cmp x20, #AST_LABEL_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f1
    cmp x20, #AST_DEFAULT_STMT
    b.eq .Lx86ps2_f0
    b .Lx86ps2_next
.Lx86ps2_if:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]          // then branch
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]          // else branch
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
    b .Lx86ps2_next
.Lx86ps2_for:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]          // init clause
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // body
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
    b .Lx86ps2_next
.Lx86ps2_f1:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F1]
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
    b .Lx86ps2_next
.Lx86ps2_f0:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
.Lx86ps2_next:
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F4]         // next sibling
    b .Lx86ps2_loop
.Lx86ps2_ret:
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_emit_statics() — walk every function body for static locals.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_statics:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_tu@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86es2_done
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F0]         // first top-level declaration
.Lx86es2_loop:
    cbz x19, .Lx86es2_done
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x0, #AST_FUNC_DECL
    b.ne .Lx86es2_next
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // function body
    cbz x0, .Lx86es2_next
    bl _kcc_x86_prereg_stmt
.Lx86es2_next:
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F4]
    b .Lx86es2_loop
.Lx86es2_done:
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Read-Only Initialised Data: const Globals}
\label{sec:x86-rodata}

A \texttt{const} global with a fixed initialiser is realised as bytes in a
read-only section rather than as zeroed storage plus a runtime store---the IR
carries no store for it, so the value must come from the object image.  For a
scalar or a one-dimensional array of scalars, the byte image is just the
initialiser's elements laid out end to end, which the textual back end emits
directly as value directives: \texttt{.byte} for a one-byte element,
\texttt{.short} for two, \texttt{.long} for four, \texttt{.quad} for eight.
Each element's bits come from the shared \texttt{\_kcc\_rodata\_leaf\_bits},
the same evaluator the ARM64 back end uses, so a constant expression, a
negation, or a folded arithmetic initialiser all reduce to a number here.  An
array longer than its initialiser has its tail zero-filled with a single
\texttt{.zero} directive.

A \texttt{const} struct of scalar fields, and a one-dimensional array of such
structs, are laid out field by field.  A struct's initialiser matches its
fields positionally, but a field does not always begin where the previous one
ended---the ABI inserts padding so each member sits at its natural alignment---%
so \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_struct\_bytes} tracks a write cursor and, before
each field, emits a \texttt{.zero} run to bridge any gap from the cursor to that
field's recorded byte offset.  Each field is then a scalar value directive at
its own width, and any bytes past the last initialised field (trailing padding
or unmentioned fields) are zero-filled to the struct's total size.  An array of
structs repeats this per element and zero-fills any element the initialiser
omits.  The only \texttt{const} aggregate still declined is a pointer
array---its elements are relocations to other symbols, not plain bytes---which
stays undefined, an honest link error.  The routine returns~1 when it emitted
the data.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_struct_bytes(type *sty, node *init, uint64_t total) — lay out
//    one struct's scalar fields as value directives, bridging alignment padding
//    with .zero and zero-filling the tail to `total` bytes.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_struct_bytes:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-80]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    str x25, [sp, #64]
    mov x22, x2                     // total struct size
    ldr x0, [x0, #TY_F0]            // AST_STRUCT_DECL
    ldr x19, [x0, #AST_F1]          // field cursor
    mov x20, #0                     // init element cursor
    cbz x1, .Lx86sb_fields
    ldr x20, [x1, #AST_F0]          // first init element
.Lx86sb_fields:
    mov x23, #0                     // write cursor (byte position)
.Lx86sb_loop:
    cbz x19, .Lx86sb_tail
    // Field type → size + kind; field byte offset.
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    cbz x0, .Lx86sb_tail
    ldr x24, [x0, #TY_SIZE]         // field size
    ldr x25, [x0, #TY_KIND]        // field kind
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]         // field byte offset
    // Bridge any alignment padding [cursor, offset) with .zero.
    cmp x0, x23
    b.ls .Lx86sb_atfield
    sub x1, x0, x23                // gap bytes
    mov x23, x0                    // cursor = offset
    mov x0, x1
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_zero
.Lx86sb_atfield:
    // Value bits: from the init element if present, else zero.
    cbz x20, .Lx86sb_zeroval
    mov x0, x20
    mov x1, x25
    bl _kcc_rodata_leaf_bits
    ldr x20, [x20, #AST_F4]        // advance init element
    b .Lx86sb_emit
.Lx86sb_zeroval:
    mov x0, #0
.Lx86sb_emit:
    mov x1, x24                    // field size
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_value
    add x23, x23, x24             // cursor += field size
    ldr x19, [x19, #AST_F4]        // next field
    b .Lx86sb_loop
.Lx86sb_tail:
    // Zero-fill from the cursor to the struct's total size.
    cmp x23, x22
    b.ge .Lx86sb_done
    sub x0, x22, x23
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_zero
.Lx86sb_done:
    ldr x25, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #80
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_emit_zero(uint64_t n) — emit "\t.zero n\n" (nothing if n == 0).
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_zero:
    cbz x0, .Lx86ez_ret
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x19, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldr x19, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
.Lx86ez_ret:
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_emit_rodata_global(node *decl, const char *name) → 1 emitted, 0 declined.
//    Emits a const global's read-only byte image: scalar, array-of-scalar,
//    struct-of-scalars, or array of such structs.  Declines a pointer array.
//    `name` is the linker symbol: the source name for a file-scope global, the
//    mangled sl_<node> for a static local.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_rodata_global:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-96]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    stp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    str x27, [sp, #80]
    mov x19, x0                     // decl
    mov x20, x1                     // symbol name (source name, or mangled sl_ for a static local)
    cbz x20, .Lx86rg_decline

    // Resolve type and classify the shape into x22:
    //   0 = scalar, 1 = array of scalar, 2 = struct, 3 = array of struct.
    // x23 = the element/struct type; x25 = element size; x26 = element kind;
    // x24 = declared element count (for the array shapes).
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F0]
    bl _kcc_sema_resolve_type
    cbz x0, .Lx86rg_decline
    mov x21, x0                     // full type
    ldr x0, [x21, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x0, #TY_STRUCT
    b.eq .Lx86rg_shape_struct
    cmp x0, #TY_ARRAY
    b.eq .Lx86rg_shape_array
    // Scalar.
    mov x22, #0
    mov x23, x21
    mov x24, #1
    b .Lx86rg_classified
.Lx86rg_shape_struct:
    mov x22, #2
    mov x23, x21                    // struct type
    b .Lx86rg_classified
.Lx86rg_shape_array:
    ldr x23, [x21, #TY_F0]          // element type
    ldr x0, [x23, #TY_KIND]
    cmp x0, #TY_STRUCT
    b.eq .Lx86rg_shape_aos
    mov x22, #1                     // array of scalar
    b .Lx86rg_arr_count
.Lx86rg_shape_aos:
    mov x22, #3                     // array of struct
.Lx86rg_arr_count:
    ldr x25, [x23, #TY_SIZE]        // element size
    cbz x25, .Lx86rg_decline
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_type_layout             // total bytes
    udiv x24, x0, x25               // declared element count = total / elem_size
.Lx86rg_classified:
    ldr x25, [x23, #TY_SIZE]        // element size (bytes)
    ldr x26, [x23, #TY_KIND]        // element kind (for leaf_bits)

    // Read-only section header + alignment, then .globl / label (once).
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbz x0, .Lx86rg_dsect
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rodata@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rodata@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86rg_sect
.Lx86rg_dsect:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_constsect@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_constsect@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86rg_sect:
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_p2align3@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_p2align3@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // .globl <name>  (unless static)
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F3]          // storage class
    cmp x0, #3                      // static → internal linkage
    b.eq .Lx86rg_label
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_globl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
.Lx86rg_label:
    mov x0, x20
    bl _kcc_x86_sym
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_colon_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str

    // Dispatch by shape.
    cmp x22, #2
    b.eq .Lx86rg_struct
    cmp x22, #3
    b.eq .Lx86rg_aos

    // Scalar / array-of-scalar: one value directive per element.  x22 = emitted.
    mov x22, #0
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]          // initialiser
    cbz x0, .Lx86rg_tail            // no init → all zero (tail fill covers it)
    ldr x1, [x0, #AST_TAG]
    cmp x1, #AST_INIT_LIST
    b.ne .Lx86rg_scalar_init
    ldr x27, [x0, #AST_F0]          // first element
.Lx86rg_elem_loop:
    cbz x27, .Lx86rg_tail
    mov x0, x27
    mov x1, x26                     // element kind
    bl _kcc_rodata_leaf_bits        // x0 = element bits
    mov x1, x25                     // element size
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_value          // emit ".<width> <masked value>\n"
    add x22, x22, #1
    ldr x27, [x27, #AST_F4]
    b .Lx86rg_elem_loop
.Lx86rg_scalar_init:
    ldr x0, [x19, #AST_F2]          // the scalar init node
    mov x1, x26
    bl _kcc_rodata_leaf_bits
    mov x1, x25
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_value
    mov x22, #1
.Lx86rg_tail:
    // Zero-fill any declared elements the initialiser did not cover.
    cmp x22, x24
    b.ge .Lx86rg_ok
    sub x0, x24, x22
    mul x0, x0, x25                 // (count - emitted) * elem_size bytes
    cbz x0, .Lx86rg_ok
    mov x27, x0                     // byte count
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    b .Lx86rg_ok

.Lx86rg_struct:
    // One struct: lay out its fields with padding.  total size = layout.
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_type_layout
    mov x24, x0                     // total struct size
    mov x0, x23                     // struct type
    ldr x1, [x19, #AST_F2]          // init list
    mov x2, x24                     // total size
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_struct_bytes
    b .Lx86rg_ok
.Lx86rg_aos:
    // Array of structs: each initialiser element is a struct brace list.
    // x23 = struct element type, x25 = struct size, x24 = declared count.
    mov x22, #0                     // emitted struct count
    ldr x27, [x19, #AST_F2]         // array init list
    cbz x27, .Lx86rg_aos_tail
    ldr x27, [x27, #AST_F0]         // first struct element
.Lx86rg_aos_loop:
    cbz x27, .Lx86rg_aos_tail
    cmp x22, x24
    b.ge .Lx86rg_aos_tail
    mov x0, x23                     // struct type
    mov x1, x27                     // this struct's brace list
    mov x2, x25                     // struct size
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_struct_bytes
    add x22, x22, #1
    ldr x27, [x27, #AST_F4]         // next struct element
    b .Lx86rg_aos_loop
.Lx86rg_aos_tail:
    // Zero-fill missing array elements.
    cmp x22, x24
    b.ge .Lx86rg_ok
    sub x0, x24, x22
    mul x0, x0, x25
    cbz x0, .Lx86rg_ok
    mov x27, x0
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zerodir@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x27
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch

.Lx86rg_ok:
    mov x0, #1
    b .Lx86rg_ret
.Lx86rg_decline:
    mov x0, #0
.Lx86rg_ret:
    ldr x27, [sp, #80]
    ldp x25, x26, [sp, #64]
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #96
    ret

// -- kcc_x86_emit_value(uint64_t bits, uint64_t size) — emit one value
//    directive (.byte/.short/.long/.quad) with `bits` masked to `size` bytes.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_value:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    mov x19, x0                     // bits
    mov x20, x1                     // size
    cmp x20, #1
    b.eq .Lx86ev_b
    cmp x20, #2
    b.eq .Lx86ev_s
    cmp x20, #8
    b.eq .Lx86ev_q
    // default: 4-byte .long
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_long@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_long@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    and x19, x19, #0xffffffff
    b .Lx86ev_num
.Lx86ev_b:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_byte@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_byte@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    and x19, x19, #0xff
    b .Lx86ev_num
.Lx86ev_s:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_short@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_short@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    and x19, x19, #0xffff
    b .Lx86ev_num
.Lx86ev_q:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_quad@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_d_quad@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
.Lx86ev_num:
    mov x0, x19
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@}

\section{Registering the Module Initialiser}
\label{sec:x86-modinit}

A file-scope initialiser that is not a compile-time constant---and, as it
happens, a \texttt{static} array initialised from a string literal, which
\kcc{} fills with runtime stores rather than read-only bytes---is emitted as
code inside a synthesised \texttt{kcc.module\_init} function.  That function
only runs if something \emph{calls} it, and in a \texttt{main}-bearing unit its
work is inlined into \texttt{main}; but in every other translation unit it must
be registered so the loader runs it before \texttt{main}.  When the IR builder
recorded a module initialiser (\texttt{\_kcc\_modinit\_present}),
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_modinit} emits a pointer to it in the platform's
init-array section---\texttt{\_\_DATA,\_\_mod\_init\_func} on Darwin,
\texttt{.init\_array} on Linux---so a global or static's initialiser in a
non-\texttt{main} unit actually executes.  Without this the storage stays
zero, which is exactly why a freshly linked zlib reported a bogus version:
\texttt{deflateInit}'s \texttt{static const char my\_version[] = ZLIB\_VERSION}
was never filled.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_modinit() — register kcc.module_init in the init-array.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_modinit:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
    mov x29, sp
    adrp x0, _kcc_modinit_present@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_modinit_present@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbz x0, .Lx86mi_done
    // Section header (Darwin __mod_init_func vs Linux .init_array), then
    // ".quad <kcc.module_init>".
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbz x0, .Lx86mi_darwin
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinit_l@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinit_l@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86mi_emit
.Lx86mi_darwin:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinit_d@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinit_d@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86mi_emit:
    bl _kcc_as_str
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinitnm@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_modinitnm@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_x86_sym                 // "_kcc.module_init" (Darwin) / bare (Linux)
    mov x0, #10
    bl _kcc_as_ch
.Lx86mi_done:
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #16
    ret
@}

\section{Emitting String-Literal Data}
\label{sec:x86-strings}

A string literal reaches the back end as an \texttt{IR\_STRING\_LIT} whose
\texttt{SRC1} points at the raw bytes and \texttt{SRC2} is their length.
Selection cannot emit the bytes inline---they belong in the read-only
\texttt{\_\_TEXT,\_\_cstring} section, not in the instruction stream---so it
does the same two-phase trick the ARM64 back end does: at the use site it
assigns the literal the next private label \verb|.Lx86str|$N$, records the
\texttt{(bytes,\,length)} pair in a table, and loads the label's address with a
\texttt{leaq}.  After all functions and globals are emitted,
\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_emit\_strings} walks the table once and emits the cstring
section: each entry's label followed by a \texttt{.byte} line carrying its
bytes and a terminating NUL.  Emitting raw \texttt{.byte} values (rather than a
quoted \texttt{.asciz}) sidesteps any escaping question---an embedded quote,
backslash, or NUL is just another number.  The table is per-render state,
initialised in \texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_render}; the label is private
(\verb|.L|-prefixed), so two translation units never collide.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_emit_strings() — emit the cstring section for recorded literals.
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_emit_strings:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-64]!
    mov x29, sp
    stp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    stp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    stp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x19, [x0]
    cbz x19, .Lx86es_done
    ldr x20, [x19, #VEC_COUNT]      // number of literals
    cbz x20, .Lx86es_done

    // Read-only section header: Darwin __TEXT,__cstring vs Linux .rodata.
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_os@@PAGEOFF
    ldr x0, [x0]
    cbz x0, .Lx86es_darwin_sect
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_rodata@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_rodata@@PAGEOFF
    b .Lx86es_sect_emit
.Lx86es_darwin_sect:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_cstrsect@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_cstrsect@@PAGEOFF
.Lx86es_sect_emit:
    bl _kcc_as_str

    mov x21, #0                     // literal index
.Lx86es_lit:
    cmp x21, x20
    b.ge .Lx86es_done
    // .Lx86str<id>:
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_strlbl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x0, x21
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_label_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    // Fetch entry {ptr, len}.
    mov x0, x19
    mov x1, x21
    bl _kcc_vec_get
    ldr x23, [x0]                   // data ptr
    ldr x24, [x0, #8]               // length
    // \t.byte
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_byte@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_byte@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    mov x22, #0                     // byte index
.Lx86es_byte:
    cmp x22, x24
    b.ge .Lx86es_nul
    ldrb w0, [x23, x22]
    bl _kcc_as_dec
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_comma1@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    add x22, x22, #1
    b .Lx86es_byte
.Lx86es_nul:
    // trailing NUL terminator, then newline
    adrp x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_nl@@PAGE
    add  x0, x0, _kcc_x86_s_zero_nl@@PAGEOFF
    bl _kcc_as_str
    add x21, x21, #1
    b .Lx86es_lit
.Lx86es_done:
    ldp x23, x24, [sp, #48]
    ldp x21, x22, [sp, #32]
    ldp x19, x20, [sp, #16]
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #64
    ret
@}

\section{Rendering to a File}
\label{sec:x86-render}

\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_render(path)} is the entry point the driver calls.  It
reuses the growable text buffer of the \texttt{.s} back end
(\S\ref{sec:emit-asm-buffer})---\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_init} to allocate it and
\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_str}/\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_ch}/\texttt{\_kcc\_as\_dec} to fill
it---renders the functions and then the global data, then writes the buffer to
the output path through the shared \texttt{\_kcc\_as\_write\_file} helper,
exactly as the ARM64 assembly path does.  The buffer machinery is
target-neutral; only the text poured into it is x86-64.

@d X86: render @{
// -- kcc_x86_render(const char *path) — render x86-64 asm to <path>. 0 on ok.
.globl _kcc_x86_render
.p2align 2
_kcc_x86_render:
    stp x29, x30, [sp, #-32]!
    mov x29, sp
    str x0, [sp, #16]              // save path across the render calls
    bl _kcc_as_init
    // Fresh string-literal table for this render (16-byte {ptr,len} entries).
    mov x0, #16
    bl _kcc_vec_init
    adrp x1, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGE
    add  x1, x1, _kcc_x86_strs@@PAGEOFF
    str x0, [x1]
    bl _kcc_x86_render_text        // emits functions (records string literals)
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_data          // file-scope global storage
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_statics       // static-local storage (mangled sl_ names)
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_strings       // cstring section for recorded literals
    bl _kcc_x86_emit_modinit       // register kcc.module_init (non-const inits)
    ldr x0, [sp, #16]             // path
    bl _kcc_as_write_file
    ldp x29, x30, [sp], #32
    ret
@| _kcc_x86_render _kcc_target _kcc_x86_tu
@}

\section{Rung One in Retrospect}
\label{sec:x86-rung1-history}

Rung~one selected only \texttt{IR\_CONST} and \texttt{IR\_RET} and carried the
returned constant in a small per-function value map, emitting the whole
function as \verb|movl $N, %eax| / \verb|retq| with no stack frame at all.
Rung~two subsumes that path: a constant now materialises into its vreg slot
and the return loads from it, so \verb|int main(void){return N;}| still exits
with \texttt{N}---now through the general all-spill frame rather than a
special case.  The map is gone; every value lives in memory between
instructions, which is exactly what let the arithmetic and memory opcodes join
the target without disturbing the ones already working.
